<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>For each pair of loci, decide how many points they share.</p><ol><li><p>Two circles of radii <m>{6}</m> cm and <m>{8}</m> cm whose centres are <m>{8}</m> cm apart</p><div>[ no points | exactly one point | two points ]  </div></li><li><p>A circle of radius <m>{5}</m> cm and a straight line <m>{6}</m> cm from its centre</p><div>[ no points | exactly one point | two points ]  </div></li><li><p>Two circles of radii <m>{9}</m> cm and <m>{3}</m> cm whose centres are <m>{2}</m> cm apart</p><div>[ no points | exactly one point | two points ]  </div></li><li><p>Two circles of radii <m>{7}</m> cm and <m>{3}</m> cm whose centres are <m>{10}</m> cm apart</p><div>[ no points | exactly one point | two points ]  </div></li></ol></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Two circles of radii <m>r_1</m> and <m>r_2</m> with centres <m>d</m> apart meet at two points when <m>|r_1 - r_2| \lt d \lt r_1 + r_2</m>, at one point when <m>d = r_1 + r_2</m> or <m>d = |r_1 - r_2|</m>, and not at all otherwise. A circle of radius <m>r</m> and a line <m>p</m> from its centre meet twice when <m>p \lt r</m>, once (a tangent) when <m>p = r</m>, and not at all when <m>p \gt r</m>.</p><ol><li><p>The distance between the centres, <m>{8}</m> cm, is between the difference of the radii, <m>{2}</m> cm, and their sum, <m>{14}</m> cm, so the circles cross twice.</p></li><li><p>The distance from the centre to the line, <m>{6}</m> cm, is more than the radius, <m>{5}</m> cm: the line misses the circle.</p></li><li><p>The centres are <m>{2}</m> cm apart, less than <m>{9} + {3}</m>, but also less than the difference of the radii, <m>{6}</m> cm: the smaller circle is inside the larger one.</p></li><li><p><m>{10} = {7} + {3}</m> exactly, so the circles just touch at one point.</p></li></ol></p></solution>
    <answer><p>two points</p></answer>
    <answer><p>no points</p></answer>
    <answer><p>no points</p></answer>
    <answer><p>exactly one point</p></answer>
</stack-static>